      "scientificChange": {
        "before": "A light floating object, such as a matchbox, mainly moves up and down near its original position when there is no significant current or wind and the wave amplitude is small, instead of continually traveling outward with a crest.\nThis approximate observation distinguishes the object's local motion from the propagation of the pattern; it does not rule out small horizontal motions or drift associated with water waves.",
        "after": "A light floating object, such as a matchbox, can move locally near its original position with both vertical and horizontal components when there is no significant current or wind and the wave amplitude is small, rather than continually propagating outward like a crest.\nThe relative sizes of its vertical and horizontal motions depend on water depth, wavelength, and the object's response; wave-induced drift can also occur, so neither vertical dominance nor relatively small horizontal motion can be assumed in general."
      },
